A: First, we'll analyze $P_1$.  Let the vertices of $P_1$ be $A_1,A_2,\dots,A_{33}$.  Each vertex is the midpoint of exactly two sides of $P_2$, so the sum of the $x$-coordinates of the vertices of $P_2$ is the same as the sum of the $x$-coordinates of the vertices of $P_1$.  Similarly, the sum of the $x$-coordinates of the vertices of $P_3$ is the same as the sum of the $x$-coordinates of the vertices of $P_2$.  Therefore, the sum of the $x$-coordinates of the vertices of $P_3$ is the same as the sum of the $x$-coordinates of the vertices of $P_1$.  So, we just need to find the sum of the $x$-coordinates of the vertices of $P_1$.

Let the coordinates of the vertices of $P_1$ be $(x_1,y_1)$, $(x_2,y_2)$, $\dots$, $(x_{33},y_{33})$.  The sum of the $x$-coordinates is then $x_1+x_2+\cdots+x_{33}$.  The sum of the $y$-coordinates is then $y_1+y_2+\cdots+y_{33}$.

[asy]
